Credentials Management:

    Jenkins provides a Credentials plugin to securely store secrets.

    Secret Text

    Store API tokens or secret strings.

Usage:

groovy

withCredentials([string(credentialsId: 'my-secret', variable: 'TOKEN')]) {
    sh 'curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" https://api.example.com'
}

--------------------------------------------------------------


Username/Password

    Store login credentials.

Usage:

groovy

withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: 'my-creds', usernameVariable: 'USER', passwordVariable: 'PASS')]) {
    sh 'echo $USER && echo $PASS'
}

----------------------------------------------------------

SSH Keys

    Store private keys for Git/remote server access.

Usage:

groovy

withCredentials([sshUserPrivateKey(credentialsId: 'ssh-key', keyFileVariable: 'KEYFILE')]) {
    sh 'ssh -i $KEYFILE user@server'
}


---------------------------------------------

AWS Credentials

Store AWS access keys.

Usage:

groovy

withCredentials([aws(credentialsId: 'aws-creds', accessKeyVariable: 'AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID', secretKeyVariable: 'AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY')]) {
    sh 'aws s3 ls'
}

-------------------------------------------------------------

Secure Secret Usage

***** Never hardcode credentials in scripts or Jenkinsfiles. *****

Always use withCredentials blocks.

Avoid echoing secrets in logs (echo $PASS is unsafe).

Use environment variables only inside secure blocks.

Prefer Jenkins credentials binding over passing secrets directly to shell commands.


